-- title:
--   Crossing presentation of the node ring at level one
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $q$, a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{red}\colon A\to k$, modular polynomial data `data` for $q$ satisfying the Kronecker congruence $hKr$ (its bivariate reduction mod $q$ equals $(Y^q-X)(Y-X^q)$), integrality hypotheses $h\alpha_1,h\beta_1$ for the two Hecke embeddings at level $1$ and prime $q$, a place specialisation $P_1$ for these data, and a prolongation tuple $R$ over $P_1$. Assume $q\ge 5$; that $R$ is a model (the two divisor laws and the cusp laws at $\infty$ and at $0$); that $W$ is a finite set of places of the level-one geometric modular function field $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,k\,1$, each supersingular (rational, affine for $j$ and $j_N$, with $j$-value in $\mathrm{ssJSet}\,q\,k$); and that $R$ satisfies the regularity law and the node value law for $W$. Let $K$ be a finite extension of $\mathbb Q$ inside $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, put $B:=A\cap K$ (the subring $\mathrm{coeffSubring}\,A\,K$), and let $w\in W$ have $j$-value $a=w.\mathrm{evalAt}(\mathrm{jGeomGen}\,k\,1)$. Assume $a$ is lifted by some $x\in B$, i.e. the restricted reduction $B\to k$ sends $x$ to $a$, and that $\varpi\in B$ generates the kernel of that reduction: $d\mapsto 0$ iff $\varpi\mid d$. Write $S:=R.\mathrm{nodeIntegersOver}\,K\,w$, the subring of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}(1\cdot q)$ of functions integral for $R_1$, for $R_2$ and for every place of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ above $w$ under $P_1.\mathrm{reduceFst}$, whose Laurent expansion lies in the subfield generated by constants from $K$ together with $j$ and $j_{q}$; and $\pi:=R.\mathrm{nodeConst}\,K\,w\,\varpi\in S$, the constant function $\varpi$. Let $c_0$ be a pair of node coordinates at $w$ over $K$, i.e. $x_0,y_0\in S$ with first residue of $x_0$ zero, $\mathrm{ord}$ of the second residue of $x_0$ at the Frobenius translate $\mathrm{arithFrobC}\,q\,k\,1\cdot w$ equal to $1$, second residue of $y_0$ zero and $\mathrm{ord}_w$ of the first residue of $y_0$ equal to $1$. The conclusion asserts the existence of node coordinates $c=(x',y')$ at $w$ over $K$ with the same branch ideals, $(\pi,x')=(\pi,x_0)$ and $(\pi,y')=(\pi,y_0)$; of $e_K\ge 1$ and a unit $\varepsilon$ of $B$ with $q=\varpi^{e_K}\varepsilon$ in $B$; and of $E\ge 1$ and a unit $u$ of $S$ with $x'y'=\pi^{E}u$, such that $(\pi,x',y')$ is a maximal ideal of $S$ and is the only maximal ideal of $S$, the ideals $(\pi,x')$ and $(\pi,y')$ are prime, $y'\notin(\pi,x')$ and $x'\notin(\pi,y')$.
--
--   This is the local crossing (ordinary double point) presentation of the integral model of $X_0(q)$ at a supersingular point of the characteristic-$q$ fibre, in the level-one case and in the form of a normalised equation $x'y'=\pi^{E}u$ in the node ring over a number field $K$ containing a lift of the $j$-invariant. It is used downstream in the construction of node packages, in the production of inertia-fixed node coordinates, and in the computation of the component group contribution at such a node.
